## 34. Professional Developer Recommendations (Production Readiness)

The sections above define the product experience. To make this a genuinely production-grade, business-viable, portfolio-worthy build, also plan for the following.

### 34.1 Business & Conversion

- Lead capture / dealer handoff: a "Request Quote" or "Book a Test Drive" flow tied to a saved build.
- Reservation/checkout stub (e.g. Stripe "Reserve with Deposit") so the product feels like real commerce, not just a showcase.
- Admin/CMS panel for non-technical staff to add vehicles, edit prices/options, and swap 3D assets without a code deploy.

### 34.2 Engineering Hygiene

- Automated testing: unit tests for pricing logic, integration tests for AI response validation, E2E tests for the configure → save → share flow.
- CI/CD pipeline with lint/type-check/test/build gates and preview deployments.
- Error monitoring (e.g. Sentry) on both frontend and backend — critical for hard-to-reproduce WebGL/3D bugs.
- Structured backend logging, especially around AI calls and pricing calculations.
- `.env.example` and setup documentation so the project is runnable by others.

### 34.3 SEO & Growth

- On-page SEO: meta tags, Open Graph/Twitter cards (ideally a dynamic preview image per shared build), sitemap, structured data (schema.org Vehicle/Product).
- Analytics/funnel tracking across landing → configure → save → share/quote to measure real engagement.

### 34.4 Compliance & Trust

- Cookie consent / privacy policy, required given EU (€) pricing and any analytics/AI logging.
- GDPR-compliant handling of any personal data attached to saved builds or leads.

### 34.5 Resilience for the 3D Experience

- WebGL capability detection with a graceful static/2D fallback for unsupported devices/browsers.
- Asset versioning and CDN cache strategy for GLB files so updates don't break previously shared configuration IDs.

### 34.6 Polish

- i18n readiness: locale-aware currency/number formatting at minimum, structured for future language support.
- Public API documentation (e.g. minimal OpenAPI/Swagger) — useful operationally and as portfolio evidence of API design quality.

